Buying the ticket costs $2$ in every case. On a win the net gain is $10 - 2 = 8$, which happens with probability $\frac{1}{20}$, and on a loss the net gain is $-2$, which happens with probability $\frac{19}{20}$.

E[\text{net}] = \frac{1}{20} \cdot 8 + \frac{19}{20} \cdot (-2) = \frac{8 - 38}{20} = -\frac{30}{20} = -\frac{3}{2}

The ticket loses a dollar fifty on average per play, so the ticket is worth less than its price.
